1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,647
The conditions that cause a production
incident —

2
00:00:05,647 --> 00:00:07,499
a node failure,
a bad deploy,

3
00:00:07,499 --> 00:00:10,464
memory exhaustion —
are the same conditions that kill the

4
00:00:10,464 --> 00:00:12,078
agent responding to it.

5
00:00:12,078 --> 00:00:16,143
And an agent that holds its state in
memory doesn't degrade gracefully.

6
00:00:16,143 --> 00:00:17,404
It stops.

7
00:00:17,404 --> 00:00:20,123
Then a human restarts the incident from
zero,

8
00:00:20,123 --> 00:00:23,112
with no idea which remediation actions
already ran.

9
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:29,051
Continuum is an incident-response agent
whose memory lives in CockroachDB,

10
00:00:29,051 --> 00:00:30,740
not in the process.

11
00:00:30,740 --> 00:00:33,970
On AWS Lambda,
it never trusts what's in memory —

12
00:00:33,970 --> 00:00:37,768
every invocation re-reads its state from
the database first.

13
00:00:37,768 --> 00:00:39,956
So the process is allowed to die.

14
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:44,442
Five agents,
and only one of them is allowed to write.

15
00:00:44,442 --> 00:00:48,628
Every fact about an incident goes through
a single module into one database —

16
00:00:48,628 --> 00:00:52,496
so whatever picks that incident up next
can trust everything it reads.

17
00:00:53,500 --> 00:00:55,080
An alert fires.

18
00:00:55,080 --> 00:00:59,439
Bedrock turns it into a vector,
CockroachDB finds the closest incident

19
00:00:59,439 --> 00:01:02,647
it's seen before,
Claude proposes the next step.

20
00:01:02,647 --> 00:01:06,210
And here's what matters —
the step is written down as executing

21
00:01:06,210 --> 00:01:08,092
before it runs,
not after.

22
00:01:09,100 --> 00:01:10,227
Now watch.

23
00:01:10,227 --> 00:01:12,311
The process is killed mid-step.

24
00:01:12,311 --> 00:01:13,999
No graceful shutdown.

25
00:01:13,999 --> 00:01:15,302
No checkpoint.

26
00:01:15,302 --> 00:01:17,044
Nothing gets a chance to clean up.

27
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:23,862
The process is gone.

28
00:01:23,862 --> 00:01:26,559
The step is still there —
sitting in executing,

29
00:01:26,559 --> 00:01:28,619
with nothing alive that owns it.

30
00:01:28,619 --> 00:01:31,912
That row is the agent's memory,
and it outlived the agent.

31
00:01:35,900 --> 00:01:38,641
A cold Lambda invocation —
a different machine,

32
00:01:38,641 --> 00:01:41,339
in a different region,
with no memory of this.

33
00:01:41,339 --> 00:01:44,704
It reads CockroachDB first,
finds the interrupted step,

34
00:01:44,704 --> 00:01:46,131
and re-runs it.

35
00:01:46,131 --> 00:01:47,767
Not from scratch.

36
00:01:47,767 --> 00:01:49,031
Not skipped.

37
00:01:49,031 --> 00:01:50,204
Not duplicated.

38
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:54,194
That isn't one lucky take.

39
00:01:54,194 --> 00:01:56,355
Fifty interrupted incidents.

40
00:01:56,355 --> 00:01:58,182
Fifty clean resumes.

41
00:01:58,182 --> 00:02:00,937
Zero duplicated actions,
zero lost steps —

42
00:02:00,937 --> 00:02:03,888
counted from the durable rows,
not from a log.

43
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:07,097
And it isn't only our own kill switch.

44
00:02:07,097 --> 00:02:10,084
Here,
AWS terminates the function itself,

45
00:02:10,084 --> 00:02:13,406
mid-step,
with no signal the process can catch.

46
00:02:13,406 --> 00:02:16,020
All fifteen recovered,
exactly once.

47
00:02:16,600 --> 00:02:18,797
And the memory scales with it.

48
00:02:18,797 --> 00:02:21,583
From one hundred incidents to ten
thousand,

49
00:02:21,583 --> 00:02:25,629
CockroachDB's vector index stays flat
while a full scan climbs away —

50
00:02:25,629 --> 00:02:27,784
seven and a half times faster.

51
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:32,082
And because it's all one database,
you can simply ask it —

52
00:02:32,082 --> 00:02:34,736
the app querying its own memory,
live,

53
00:02:34,736 --> 00:02:37,544
through CockroachDB's managed MCP server.

54
00:02:38,100 --> 00:02:40,688
Type-checked,
linted and gated in CI,

55
00:02:40,688 --> 00:02:45,521
with the recovery contract pinned by tests
that hard-kill a real process on every

56
00:02:45,521 --> 00:02:46,476
push.

57
00:02:47,100 --> 00:02:49,451
Agents will keep dying mid-task.

58
00:02:49,451 --> 00:02:52,788
Continuum is the one that picks up exactly
where it left off.
